<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swedish–Norwegian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Swedish–Norwegian War was a short war in July and August 1814 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, which had declared independence after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kiel">Treaty of Kiel</a> transferred it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> to Sweden. Swedish forces under Crown Prince Charles John (Bernadotte) quickly gained the upper hand, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-moss">Convention of Moss</a> ended the fighting. Norway kept its new constitution but entered a personal union with Sweden that lasted until 1905.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swedish-norwegian-war.jpg" alt="Swedish–Norwegian War" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oscar Wergeland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eidsvoll_riksraad_1814.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kiel">Treaty of Kiel</a>, signed on 13 January 1814, King Frederick VI of Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> had to cede Norway to the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, the two kingdoms to enter a union. Swedish Crown Prince Charles John (Karl Johan) had arranged Russian support for such an attack as early as 1812, and in 1813 treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> had promised Norway to Sweden for its part in the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>.</p><p>The Norwegians refused to be a bargaining chip. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-viii-of-denmark">Christian Frederick</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, heir presumptive and governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, gathered a constituent assembly that adopted the liberal constitution of 17 May and named him king. He sought support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the other powers, but their diplomats, more concerned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, promised none.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Norwegian army mustered 30,000 men, while the Swedish army numbered between 45,000 and 66,800 veterans of the German campaign of 1813. Hostilities opened on 26 July with a Swedish naval attack on the Norwegian gunboats at Hvaler. The main Swedish force crossed the border at Halden and bypassed the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-fredriksten">Fredriksten</a>, while a second force of 6,000 landed at Kråkerøy, and Fredrikstad surrendered the next day.</p><p>Norwegian troops stopped the Swedes at Lier on 2 August, won at Matrand on 5 August and drove them back at Langnes on 9 August. The Swedes then outflanked the Norwegian line at the Battle of Kjølberg Bridge, opening the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Kristiania</a>, and a British blockade cut off trade and military supplies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 10 August Charles XIV John proposed a cease-fire, accepting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsvoll">Eidsvoll</a> constitution as a major concession. After negotiations in Moss, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-moss">Convention of Moss</a> was signed on 14 August. Christian Frederick was forced to abdicate, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> remained nominally independent in a personal union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> under the Swedish king. Its constitution was upheld with only the amendments the union required, and the two kingdoms kept separate institutions apart from the king and the foreign service. Many Norwegians blamed Christian Frederick and General Haxthausen, who was accused of treason, for the defeat. The war was the last in which Sweden fought another nation until 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swedish–Norwegian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
